          "message": "refactor(opam_solver): filter_deps_with_env is just filter_deps (#16479)\n\n## Description\n\nAll the calls to `filter_deps_with_env` were logically equivalent to\ncalls to `filter_deps`, and the definition of `filter_deps` introduced\nobscuring indirection and redundant data flow.\n\nClearing up the indirection of `platform_env` (which was just a record\naccess and a single function call), `filter_deps` was just\n\n```\nlet filter_deps t ~platform package filtered_formula =\n   let solver_env = Solver_env.extend t.solver_env platform in\n   filter_deps_with_env t ~solver_env package filtered_formula\n```\n\nThis shows the confusing data flow: `filter_deps_with_env` gets both a\ncontext `t` and a `solver_env`, even tho `t` already has its own\n`solver_env`. The only difference between the `t.solver_env` and the\n`~solver_env` argument is that the latter is extended with the given\nplatform.\n\nBut the only place `filter_deps_with_env` is called other than in\n`filter_deps` in\n\n```\n        let solver_env = platform_env t platform in\n        ( filter_deps_with_env t ~solver_env package local_package.depends\n        , filter_deps_with_env t ~solver_env package local_package.conflicts ))\n```\n\nWhich is just the same as two calls to `filter_deps`, modulo the\nrecomputation of the `solver_env`. The only argument for the confusing\ndata flow and redundancy would be micro-optimization that avoids\ncomputing the extended `solver_env` twice, but in the hottest path we\nare already recomputing this repeatedly (line 783) and the single extra\nconstruction here can't be worth much in comparison.\n\n## Related Issue and Motivation\n\nIdentified while resuming work on\nhttps://github.com/ocaml/dune/issues/12135\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Tests added, if applicable.\n- [x] [Change log entry\nadded](../CONTRIBUTING.md#updating-the-changelog) for any user-facing\nchanges.\n- [x] Documentation added for any user-facing changes.",
